 },
 "sources": {
  "currency": [
   "The head nouns of the currency names, singular and plural: the last word",
   "of displayName-count-one and displayName-count-other of every currency,",
   "before any \"of\" phrase and after a leading \"novo\" or \"novos\".",
   "Source: Unicode CLDR 49.0.0, cldr-json/cldr-numbers-full/main/en/currencies.json.",
   "ISO 4217 X codes (metals, funds, test codes) are left out."
  ],
  "currency_code": [
   "The ISO 4217 alphabetic codes of the currencies, in capitals: a code after a",
   "number measures it (\"three thousand USD\").",
   "Source: Unicode CLDR 49.0.0, cldr-json/cldr-numbers-full/main/en/currencies.json, the keys.",
   "ISO 4217 X codes of metals, funds and test codes are left out."
  ],
  "currency_qualifier": [
   "The words that stand between a number and a currency noun and qualify the",
   "currency: \"three thousand US dollars\", \"three thousand more dollars\".",
   "Sources: the words before the head noun of every currency name in Unicode CLDR 49.0.0,",
   "cldr-json/cldr-numbers-full/main/en/currencies.json (displayName-count-other, before any",
   "\"of\" phrase); \"more\", \"extra\" and \"additional\" as quantity adjectives, Wiktionary",
   "(en) s.v. \"more\" (adjective, \"in greater quantity, amount, or proportion\"), \"extra\"",
   "(\"additional\") and \"additional\" (\"supplemental or added to something\")."
  ],
  "daypart_morning": [
   "Plural form: Wiktionary gives \"mornings\" as the plural noun of \"morning\"",
   "and as an adverb for a regular part of the day (https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mornings),",
   "as in \"weekday mornings at 8\"."
  ],
  "marker_alarm_at": [
   "The noun of a thing that sounds at a set time, and \"for\" after it: \"an",
   "alarm for 7\", \"a reminder for 9\".  \"alarm [...] An instance of an alarm",
   "ringing, beeping or clanging, to give a noise signal at a certain time.\"",
   "(Wiktionary, s.v. \"alarm\", noun).  \"reminder [...] Someone or something",
   "that reminds.\" (Wiktionary, s.v. \"reminder\").  \"wake-up call [...] A",
   "telephone call to awaken someone at a certain time\" (Wiktionary, s.v.",
   "\"wake-up call\"; \"wakeup call\" is its alternative form).  \"for [...]",
   "Directed at; intended to belong to.\" (Wiktionary, s.v. \"for\", preposition)."
  ],
  "marker_alarm_joiner": [
   "The words that join two hours that share one later meridiem: \"alarm for 7",
   "or 8 pm\"."
  ],
  "marker_alarm_length": [
   "A word that adds to a count after the hour of \"alarm for 10\": \"alarm for 10",
   "more minutes\" is a length.  \"more [...] Additional; further.\" and \"extra",
   "[...] (beyond what is due, usual, expected, or necessary; extraneous;",
   "additional)\" (Wiktionary, s.v. \"more\", \"extra\").  \"of\" after a number",
   "names the whole the number counts from: \"alarm for 2 of you\".  \"of [...]",
   "(following a number or other quantitive word) Introduces the whole for which",
   "is indicated only the specified part or segment\" (Wiktionary, s.v. \"of\",",
   "preposition).",
   "\"h\" and \"m\" after the hour are the unit letters of a length: \"alarm for 10 h\"",
   "is ten hours and \"alarm for 10 m\" ten minutes.  \"h [...] Abbreviation of",
   "hour, particularly when used as a (non-SI) unit of time alongside SI units\"",
   "and \"m [...] Abbreviation of minute\" (Wiktionary, s.v. \"h\", \"m\", English)."
  ],
  "marker_at_counts": [
   "Clock prepositions that also introduce a counted group: a bare hour behind one",
   "is doubtful only right before a word of marker_counted_noun.voc.  Attested in",
   "the parity acceptance rows; no dictionary source checked, a native reviewer is to confirm."
  ],
  "marker_counted_noun": [
   "Nouns that count people or things a clock never takes: a bare hour behind a",
   "clock preposition and directly before one of them is a count (\"wave at 5",
   "people\").  Each word is quoted from its Wiktionary Noun entry",
   "(https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/<word>); the fetched pages are kept with the audit.",
   "people: Noun. \"plural of person: a body of persons considered generally or collectively; a group of two or more...\"",
   "person: Noun. \"An individual who has been granted personhood; usually a human being.\"",
   "persons: Noun. \"plural of person\"",
   "guests: Noun. \"plural of guest\"",
   "neighbours: Noun. \"plural of neighbour\"",
   "neighbors: Noun. \"plural of neighbor\"",
   "friends: Noun. \"plural of friend\"",
   "children: Noun. \"plural of child\"",
   "kids: Noun. \"plural of kid\"",
   "adults: Noun. \"plural of adult\"",
   "players: Noun. \"plural of player\"",
   "seats: Noun. \"plural of seat\"",
   "tables: Noun. \"plural of table\"",
   "tickets: Noun. \"plural of ticket\"",
   "rooms: Noun. \"plural of room\"",
   "houses: Noun. \"plural of house\"",
   "targets: Noun. \"plural of target\"",
   "birds: Noun. \"plural of bird\"",
   "cats: Noun. \"plural of cat\"",
   "dogs: Noun. \"plural of dog\"",
   "apples: Noun. \"plural of apple\"",
   "books: Noun. \"plural of book\"",
   "euros: Noun. \"plural of euro\"",
   "dollars: Noun. \"plural of dollar\""
  ],
  "marker_date_lead": [
   "Words that open a date or a clock time and belong to it.",
   "Each word is shown opening a time or date phrase on the page named in brackets,",
   "saved under knowledge/wiki/audits/chronologia/parity-fixes/marker-strand/sources/ in the workspace.",
   "at: Wiktionary \"at\": \"at six o’clock; at dawn; at closing time\" [en-at.wikitext.txt]",
   "on: Wiktionary \"on\": \"At or during the date or day of. On Sunday I'm busy. I'll see you on Monday.\" [en-on.wikitext.txt]"
  ],
  "marker_date_lead_no_deixis": [
   "Lead words that do not open a time expression that begins with this, next",
   "or last and names a daypart, a period or a weekday (\"on this evening\", \"at next week\",",
   "\"on next Friday\").",
   "Saved under knowledge/wiki/audits/chronologia/parity-fixes/marker-strand/sources/ in the workspace:",
   "Cambridge Dictionary, English Grammar Today, \"At, on and in (time)\": \"We don't normally use at,",
   "on or in before time expressions beginning with each, every, next, last, some, this, that, one,",
   "any, all\" [cambridge-at-on-and-in-time.txt]; its example \"Are you free next Monday at two",
   "o'clock?\" names a weekday."
  ],
  "marker_daypart_plural": [
   "Plural day-part words that a number before them turns into a count of days",
   "(\"three mornings\", \"two mornings ago\"), so they name no band.  Each is a",
   "surface of daypart_morning.voc."
  ],
  "marker_daypart_plural_after": [
   "Words after a plural day-part that make it a count of days: \"two mornings",
   "ago\", \"two mornings from now\"."
  ],
  "marker_dayparttoday": [
   "bare daypart surfaces lexically fused with TODAY: \"tonight\" already names",
   "today's night band, unlike the plain band word \"night\" (which composes",
   "with the clock's own future-roll rule -- see test_nl_r117)."
  ],
  "marker_during": [
   "\"in\" opens a calendar period as well (\"in January\", \"in the 1920s\")."
  ],
  "marker_future": [
   "\"from now\" is the postposed counterpart of the preposed \"in\": \"two hours",
   "from now\" and \"in two hours\" name the same instant.  en.wiktionary.org,",
   "entry \"from now on\", glosses \"from now\" as the present moment used as the",
   "reference point of a forward offset.  Multiword, matched as a phrase."
  ],
  "marker_holiday_indef": [
   "English indefinite articles that cannot precede a holiday name made of",
   "an adjective and a weekday: \"a good friday\" is any good Friday."
  ],
  "marker_ntolast_next": [
   "the leading word of the \"next to last\" idiom (== \"second-to-last\", -2).",
   "Deliberately a SEPARATE table from marker_next.voc's REL_MARKER \"next\"",
   "(which shifts a whole scope by +1) -- \"next to last\" is not \"next\" plus",
   "\"last\" composing an offset, it is one fixed idiom meaning -2, and reusing",
   "REL_MARKER here would let the two collide."
  ],
  "marker_ordlast_article": [
   "The article that makes \"last\" the adjective \"final\": \"the last day of",
   "school\" is the final day of school.  Bare \"last\" before a period is the",
   "determiner \"previous\", and an \"of\" after it is a verb's complement or \"of",
   "course\": \"I told you last week of the change\", \"last week of course I was",
   "busy\".  Wiktionary s.v. \"last\" (https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/last),",
   "adjective 1: \"Final, ultimate, coming after all others of its kind\";",
   "determiner 1: \"The (one) immediately before the present. We went there",
   "last year.\""
  ],
  "marker_ordlast_of": [
   "\"of\" after \"the last <unit>\": \"the last day of school\" names the final day",
   "of school, not yesterday.  Wiktionary s.v. \"last\", adjective 1: \"Final,",
   "ultimate, coming after all others of its kind\"",
   "(https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/last).  Bare \"last week\" is the",
   "determiner \"previous\" instead (``marker_ordlast_article.voc``)."
  ],
  "marker_past": [
   "\"earlier\" is the postposed backward offset, the mirror of \"ago\": \"five",
   "minutes earlier\" and \"five minutes ago\" name the same instant when the",
   "reference point is the present.  en.wiktionary.org, entry \"earlier\",",
   "comparative of \"early\", \"before the present time\"."
  ],
  "marker_penult": [
   "\"penultimate <weekday> of every month\": a single-word synonym for",
   "\"second-to-last\" -- Merriam-Webster s.v. \"penultimate\" (\"next to the last\").",
   "Always -2 (the day directly before the last); it never generalises to",
   "\"second-to-penultimate\" the way the \"<ordinal> to last\" idiom does."
  ],
  "marker_singular_only": [
   "Markers that modify a singular period only: \"the following week\" is the",
   "calendar week after, while \"the following weeks\" names no single period."
  ],
  "marker_to": [
   "the \"to\" of the \"<ordinal> to last\" / \"next to last\" idiom (\"the",
   "second-to-last friday\", \"the next-to-last friday\").  Union'd with",
   "``_RANGE_TO``'s Python-level defaults (to/until/till/through/thru) by",
   "``_conn_surfaces`` for range detection, and now also reachable as a plain",
   "grammar literal so the ``scoped_ordinal`` nth-to-last orders can match it."
  ],
